<meta http-equiv="content-type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8"></head><body><img id="imagen" src="8_Extra_Information_B_files/basecamp01.jpg" width="90%" onerror="this.style.display='none'" style="display: none;">                                                                    <p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:16.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;
mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">Embrace Diversity</span></b><b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;
mso-ansi-language:EN-GB"><o:p></o:p></span></b></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:
16.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:
EN-GB">Map out the differences in interests, power, and knowledge within your
team and make optimal use of your diversity. Learn here how to leverage this
diversity to improve collaboration and develop more creative solutions. <o:p></o:p></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:
14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:
EN-GB">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:
14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:
EN-GB">In a transdisciplinary collaboration, there are many differences among
team members. Differences are often seen as challenging, but they can also be
beneficial. By recognizing, valuing, and actively using these differences, you
can improve collaboration and outcomes. These differences can be divided into
three categories: interests, power, and knowledge (Smeenk, 2023).<o:p></o:p></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:
14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:
EN-GB">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;
mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">Diversity in Interests<o:p></o:p></span></b></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:
14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:
EN-GB">As a team member, you participate in a transdisciplinary project for
various reasons, such as personal, financial, or work-related motivations. Ask
yourself: who has which interests and why? These interests are not always
visible. If you do not articulate them, the process can become very complex and
frustrating, especially since you often work towards an unclear goal. Speak up
and help resolve conflicts constructively. <o:p></o:p></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:
14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:
EN-GB">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;
mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">Diversity in Power<o:p></o:p></span></b></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:
14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:
EN-GB">In a transdisciplinary collaboration, you almost always encounter
unequal power dynamics. A person's role, network, knowledge, or personality
partly determines their power. If these power dynamics are not acknowledged or
discussed, it can result in frustrations. However, power can also be harnessed
(Oskam, 2023). Dreaming together about the future and your common goal is the
first step. Discuss how achieving the common goal changes the position of all
parties: you want create a new playing field. This means that powerful players
must give up and change things in their habits or responsibilities: the common
goal is paramount.<o:p></o:p></span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:
14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:
EN-GB">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><b><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;
mso-ansi-language:EN-GB">Diversity in Knowledge<o:p></o:p></span></b></p><p style="margin-bottom: 16px; line-height: 1.4;">























</p><p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom:0cm"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:
14.0pt;line-height:116%;font-family:&quot;Verdana&quot;,sans-serif;mso-ansi-language:
EN-GB">The different types of knowledge that all team members bring to the
table is the foundation of your project: combining scientific and practical
insights that contribute to system knowledge (understanding the current
reality), goal knowledge (visualizing the desired outcome), and transformation
knowledge (transitioning from system knowledge to goal knowledge) (Oskam,
2023). Participants bring different kinds of knowledge from their experiences
or lifestyles. Make clear what each person's knowledge, expertise, and
experience are and what type of knowledge they can and want to contribute.<o:p></o:p></span></p>                                                                      </body></html>